 {
  "id": "q2-01-expressions-11",
  "lessonId": "q2-01-expressions",
  "type": "PS",
  "difficulty": 5,
  "stem": "If $a+b=8$ and $a^2+b^2=40$, what is the value of $a^3+b^3$?",
  "content": {
   "format": "select_one",
   "choices": [
    {
     "id": "A",
     "text": "$128$"
    },
    {
     "id": "B",
     "text": "$224$"
    },
    {
     "id": "C",
     "text": "$320$"
    },
    {
     "id": "D",
     "text": "$416$"
    },
    {
     "id": "E",
     "text": "$512$"
    }
   ]
  },
  "answer": {
   "choices": [
    "B"
   ]
  },
  "explanation": "From $(a+b)^2=a^2+2ab+b^2$: $64=40+2ab$, so $ab=12$. Then $a^3+b^3=(a+b)^3-3ab(a+b)=512-3(12)(8)=224$. (Check: $a,b=2,6$ gives $8+216$.)",
  "choiceExplanations": {
   "A": "This uses $ab=24$ (the $2ab$ was not halved).",
   "C": "This treats $a^3+b^3$ as $(a+b)(a^2+b^2)$, which omits $-ab$.",
   "D": "The factor 3 was dropped: $512-96$.",
   "E": "This is $(a+b)^3$ with the cross terms ignored."
  },
  "estSeconds": 150,
  "skills": [
   "algebraic-expressions",
   "factoring"
  ],
  "review": {
